# The sixth century (Nov 2026): the kings and saints of the Age of Arthur and the Saints who had no page yet.
# Ifan asked (12 November 2026) for "the Kings and Princes and Saints of the 6th century".
# House rules (EDITORIAL.md): proud, nothing negative about Welsh figures, tradition in its own words,
# kings are kings (every ruler of a Welsh or Old North kingdom in this age). Same entry format as people2a.py;
# people2.py gathers this list with the others. The saints match their lines in src/saints.py, which links to these pages.
from people2a import P

PEOPLE = [
 # ---------- Kings of Wales ----------
 P("cadwallon-lawhir", "saints", "fl. early 6th century", "Cadwallon Lawhir", "Cadwallon Lawhir", "King of Gwynedd, father of Maelgwn", "Brenin Gwynedd, tad Maelgwn", "Cadwallon_Lawhir_ap_Einion", "rulers",
   "King of Gwynedd, grandson of Cunedda and father of Maelgwn Gwynedd. Welsh tradition remembers him as the king who drove the Irish settlers out of Ynys Môn.",
   "Brenin Gwynedd, ŵyr Cunedda a thad Maelgwn Gwynedd. Mae traddodiad Cymru’n ei gofio fel y brenin a yrrodd y gwladfawyr Gwyddelig allan o Ynys Môn.",
   ["Cadwallon Lawhir, Cadwallon Long-Hand, was the son of Einion Yrth and grandson of Cunedda Wledig, who came from Manaw Gododdin in the North to found the royal house of Gwynedd. He reigned in the early sixth century, and his son was Maelgwn Gwynedd, the greatest king of his age.",
    "Welsh tradition holds that it was Cadwallon who won back Ynys Môn from the Irish who had settled there. The Triads remember his war band as one of the Three Fettered War-Bands of the Island of Britain: when they went into battle against Serigi the Irishman, each man bound the fetters of his horse to his own legs, so that none could turn back. Serigi fell, the Irish were driven from the island, and the place of the battle on the north coast of Môn has been called Cerrig y Gwyddyl, the Stones of the Irish.",
    "He was a giver to the saints. Tradition tells that he gave Saint Eilian as much land at Llaneilian as the saint’s tame deer could run around in a day. His name Lawhir, ‘long hand’, is said to come from an arm so long that he could pick a stone up from the ground without bending."],
   ["Mab Einion Yrth ac ŵyr Cunedda Wledig oedd Cadwallon Lawhir. Daeth Cunedda o Fanaw Gododdin yn y Gogledd i sefydlu tŷ brenhinol Gwynedd. Teyrnasodd Cadwallon yn nechrau’r chweched ganrif, a’i fab oedd Maelgwn Gwynedd, brenin mwyaf ei oes.",
    "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, Cadwallon a enillodd Ynys Môn yn ôl oddi wrth y Gwyddelod a ymsefydlasai yno. Mae’r Trioedd yn cofio ei osgordd fel un o Dair Hualog Osgordd Ynys Prydain: pan aethant i frwydr yn erbyn Serigi Wyddel, rhwymodd pob gŵr hual ei farch am ei goesau ei hun, fel na allai neb droi’n ôl. Syrthiodd Serigi, gyrrwyd y Gwyddelod o’r ynys, a gelwir man y frwydr ar arfordir gogledd Môn yn Gerrig y Gwyddyl.",
    "Bu’n hael wrth y saint. Medd y traddodiad iddo roi i Eilian Sant gymaint o dir yn Llaneilian ag y gallai carw dof y sant redeg o’i gwmpas mewn diwrnod. Dywedir i’w enw Lawhir ddod o fraich mor hir fel y gallai godi carreg o’r llawr heb blygu."]),
 P("rhun-ap-maelgwn", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Rhun ap Maelgwn", "Rhun ap Maelgwn", "King of Gwynedd who marched to the North", "Brenin Gwynedd a orymdeithiodd i’r Gogledd", "Rhun_ap_Maelgwn_Gwynedd", "rulers",
   "King of Gwynedd after his father Maelgwn. The Welsh law books tell how Rhun Hir, Rhun the Tall, led the men of Gwynedd all the way to the river Forth, and won lasting honour for the men of Arfon.",
   "Brenin Gwynedd ar ôl ei dad Maelgwn. Mae llyfrau cyfraith Cymru’n adrodd sut yr arweiniodd Rhun Hir wŷr Gwynedd yr holl ffordd i afon Gwerydd, ac ennill anrhydedd parhaol i wŷr Arfon.",
   ["Rhun son of Maelgwn became king of Gwynedd when his father died in the great plague of about 547. He was called Rhun Hir, Rhun the Tall, and Welsh tradition remembers him as a king who carried the war far beyond his borders.",
    "The story is told in the Welsh law books, in a passage on the privileges of the men of Arfon. Elidir Mwynfawr, a prince of the North, was killed at Aber Mewydus in Arfon, and the kings of the North, Clydno Eidyn, Nudd Hael, Mordaf Hael and Rhydderch Hael, came south to avenge him and burned Arfon. Rhun gathered the men of Gwynedd and marched north with them, through the lands of the Britons, as far as the banks of the Gwerydd, the river Forth.",
    "The march was so long, the law books say, that the men of Arfon were given a privilege for ever: the right to lead the van of the army of Gwynedd. Tradition holds that Caer Rhun in the Conwy valley, the Roman fort of Canovium, carries his name."],
   ["Daeth Rhun fab Maelgwn yn frenin Gwynedd pan fu farw ei dad yn y pla mawr tua 547. Fe’i gelwid yn Rhun Hir, ac mae traddodiad Cymru’n ei gofio fel brenin a gariodd y rhyfel ymhell y tu hwnt i’w ffiniau.",
    "Adroddir y stori yn llyfrau cyfraith Cymru, mewn darn am freiniau gwŷr Arfon. Lladdwyd Elidir Mwynfawr, tywysog o’r Gogledd, yn Aber Mewydus yn Arfon, a daeth brenhinoedd y Gogledd, Clydno Eidyn, Nudd Hael, Mordaf Hael a Rhydderch Hael, i’r de i’w ddial a llosgi Arfon. Casglodd Rhun wŷr Gwynedd a gorymdeithio tua’r gogledd gyda hwy, drwy diroedd y Brythoniaid, hyd at lannau Gwerydd.",
    "Roedd y daith mor hir, medd llyfrau’r gyfraith, nes rhoi braint am byth i wŷr Arfon: yr hawl i arwain blaen byddin Gwynedd. Yn ôl traddodiad, mae Caer Rhun yn Nyffryn Conwy, caer Rufeinig Canovium, yn dwyn ei enw."]),
 P("brochwel-ysgithrog", "saints", "fl. 6th century", "Brochwel Ysgithrog", "Brochwel Ysgithrog", "King of Powys", "Brenin Powys", "Brochwel_Ysgithrog", "rulers",
   "King of Powys, father of King Cynan Garwyn and of Saint Tysilio. Welsh tradition remembers him as the king who, hunting a hare, found Saint Melangell at prayer and gave her the valley of Pennant.",
   "Brenin Powys, tad y Brenin Cynan Garwyn a Tysilio Sant. Mae traddodiad Cymru’n ei gofio fel y brenin a gafodd Felangell Sant mewn gweddi wrth hela ysgyfarnog, a rhoi iddi ddyffryn Pennant.",
   ["Brochwel Ysgithrog, Brochwel of the Tusks, was king of Powys in the sixth century, of the line of Cadell Ddyrnllug. Welsh tradition places his court at Pengwern, where Shrewsbury now stands, when Powys still stretched far to the east of today’s border.",
    "His most famous hour was a hunt. Chasing a hare up the valley of the Tanat, he found it sheltering beneath the robe of a young woman at prayer in a thicket, and his hounds would not go near her. She was Melangell, a king’s daughter from Ireland who had come to live there in solitude. Brochwel was so moved that he gave her the valley as a sanctuary for ever, and Pennant Melangell has been a place of peace for both people and animals ever since; hares are still called ŵyn bach Melangell, Melangell’s little lambs.",
    "His sons were Cynan Garwyn, whose praise Taliesin sang, and Tysilio, the saint of Meifod. The kings of Powys after them, down to the house of Owain Glyndŵr, counted Brochwel among their forefathers."],
   ["Brochwel Ysgithrog oedd brenin Powys yn y chweched ganrif, o linach Cadell Ddyrnllug. Mae traddodiad Cymru’n gosod ei lys ym Mhengwern, lle saif Amwythig heddiw, pan ymestynnai Powys ymhell i’r dwyrain o’r ffin heddiw.",
    "Helfa oedd ei awr enwocaf. Wrth erlid ysgyfarnog i fyny dyffryn Tanat, cafodd hi’n llochesu dan fantell merch ifanc a oedd yn gweddïo mewn llwyn, ac ni âi ei gŵn yn agos ati. Melangell oedd hi, merch brenin o Iwerddon a ddaethai i fyw yno ar ei phen ei hun. Cyffyrddwyd Brochwel gymaint fel y rhoddodd y dyffryn iddi yn noddfa am byth, ac mae Pennant Melangell wedi bod yn lle heddwch i bobl ac anifeiliaid byth er hynny; gelwir ysgyfarnogod o hyd yn ŵyn bach Melangell.",
    "Ei feibion oedd Cynan Garwyn, y canodd Taliesin ei glod, a Thysilio, sant Meifod. Cyfrifai brenhinoedd Powys ar eu hôl, hyd at dŷ Owain Glyndŵr, Frochwel ymhlith eu cyndadau."]),
 P("cynan-garwyn", "saints", "fl. late 6th century", "Cynan Garwyn", "Cynan Garwyn", "King of Powys, praised by Taliesin", "Brenin Powys, a folwyd gan Daliesin", "Cynan_Garwyn", "rulers",
   "King of Powys, son of Brochwel Ysgithrog. Taliesin’s poem in his praise, Trawsganu Cynan Garwyn, is one of the oldest poems in Welsh.",
   "Brenin Powys, mab Brochwel Ysgithrog. Mae cerdd Taliesin i’w glodfori, Trawsganu Cynan Garwyn, yn un o’r cerddi hynaf yn y Gymraeg.",
   ["Cynan Garwyn, Cynan of the White Chariot, was king of Powys in the late sixth century, son of Brochwel Ysgithrog and brother of Saint Tysilio. He is one of the very few kings of his age whose praise has come down to us in the words of a poet who knew him.",
    "Taliesin, the chief of bards, sang Trawsganu Cynan Garwyn, ‘The Cross-Song of Cynan Garwyn’, which is kept in the Book of Taliesin. The poet praises the gifts he received at Cynan’s court, a hundred swift steeds with silver trappings, a hundred purple mantles, a hundred armlets and fifty brooches, and a sword in a jewelled scabbard. Then he sings of Cynan’s campaigns: across the Wye into Gwent, to Ynys Môn, into Dyfed and Brycheiniog. ‘Cynan, shield in battle,’ the poem calls him, the defender of his people.",
    "His son Selyf ap Cynan, Selyf Sarffgadau, ‘serpent of battles’, followed him as king of Powys and led the Britons at the great battle of Chester in 616."],
   ["Cynan Garwyn oedd brenin Powys ar ddiwedd y chweched ganrif, mab Brochwel Ysgithrog a brawd Tysilio Sant. Mae’n un o’r ychydig iawn o frenhinoedd ei oes y daeth ei glod i lawr atom yng ngeiriau bardd a’i hadwaenai.",
    "Canodd Taliesin, pen beirdd, Drawsganu Cynan Garwyn, a gedwir yn Llyfr Taliesin. Mae’r bardd yn canmol y rhoddion a gafodd yn llys Cynan, can march buan â harnais arian, can mantell borffor, can breichrwy a hanner cant o dlysau, a chleddyf mewn gwain emog. Yna mae’n canu am ymgyrchoedd Cynan: dros Wy i Went, i Ynys Môn, i Ddyfed a Brycheiniog. ‘Cynan, tarian cad,’ y gelwir ef, amddiffynnwr ei bobl.",
    "Ei fab Selyf ap Cynan, Selyf Sarffgadau, a’i dilynodd yn frenin Powys ac a arweiniodd y Brythoniaid ym mrwydr fawr Caer yn 616."]),
 P("gwrthefyr", "saints", "fl. mid 6th century", "Gwrthefyr", "Gwrthefyr", "King of Dyfed, ‘the Protector’", "Brenin Dyfed, ‘yr Amddiffynnwr’", "Vortiporius", "rulers",
   "King of Dyfed, named by Gildas, whose memorial stone, inscribed in Latin and ogam with the Roman title Protector, is one of the treasures of Carmarthen Museum.",
   "Brenin Dyfed, a enwir gan Gildas, y mae ei garreg goffa, ag arysgrif mewn Lladin ac ogam a’r teitl Rhufeinig Protector, yn un o drysorau Amgueddfa Caerfyrddin.",
   ["Gwrthefyr, Vortiporius in Latin, was king of Dyfed in the middle of the sixth century, son of Aergol Lawhir in the Welsh pedigrees. Gildas, writing in about 540, addressed him by name as the ruler of the Demetae, the people of Dyfed, and spoke of his father as a good king.",
    "His memorial stone was found at Castell Dwyran, near Clunderwen, in 1895. It is carved with a ring-cross, and with words in two alphabets: in Latin letters, MEMORIA VOTEPORIGIS PROTICTORIS, ‘the memorial of Voteporix the Protector’, and in ogam, the old Irish script of notches, his name again. Protector was a title of honour in the Roman world, and the stone shows that the kings of Dyfed still held it, generations after the legions had gone.",
    "It is one of the very few stones in Britain that may be matched to a king named in a written history of the same century. It stands today in Carmarthenshire Museum at Abergwili."],
   ["Gwrthefyr, Vortiporius mewn Lladin, oedd brenin Dyfed yng nghanol y chweched ganrif, mab Aergol Lawhir yn yr achau Cymreig. Cyfarchodd Gildas, wrth ysgrifennu tua 540, ef wrth ei enw fel rheolwr y Demetae, pobl Dyfed, a soniodd am ei dad fel brenin da.",
    "Cafwyd ei garreg goffa yng Nghastell Dwyran, ger Clunderwen, yn 1895. Mae croes mewn cylch wedi’i cherfio arni, a geiriau mewn dwy wyddor: mewn llythrennau Lladin, MEMORIA VOTEPORIGIS PROTICTORIS, ‘coffa Voteporix yr Amddiffynnwr’, ac mewn ogam, hen sgript riciau Iwerddon, ei enw eto. Teitl anrhydedd yn y byd Rhufeinig oedd Protector, ac mae’r garreg yn dangos bod brenhinoedd Dyfed yn ei ddal o hyd, genedlaethau ar ôl i’r llengoedd fynd.",
    "Mae’n un o’r ychydig iawn o feini ym Mhrydain y gellir eu cysylltu â brenin a enwir mewn hanes ysgrifenedig o’r un ganrif. Saif heddiw yn Amgueddfa Sir Gaerfyrddin yn Abergwili."]),
 P("caw-o-brydyn", "saints", "fl. late 5th century", "Caw o Brydyn", "Caw o Brydyn", "Lord of Prydyn, father of saints", "Arglwydd Prydyn, tad saint", "Caw_of_Pictland", "rulers",
   "A lord of the North, father of Gildas and of a family of saints. The Triads count his line as one of the Three Saintly Lineages of the Island of Britain, and in Culhwch ac Olwen he rides with Arthur.",
   "Arglwydd o’r Gogledd, tad Gildas a theulu o saint. Mae’r Trioedd yn cyfrif ei linach yn un o Dair Gwelygordd Saint Ynys Prydain, ac yn Culhwch ac Olwen mae’n marchogaeth gydag Arthur.",
   ["Caw of Prydyn, Caw of Prydain in some texts, was a lord of the North in the generation before the great saints. The Triads name his family, with those of Brychan Brycheiniog and Cunedda Wledig, as one of the Three Saintly Lineages of the Island of Britain, and the Welsh genealogies of the saints count more than twenty of his sons and daughters.",
    "The most famous of them was Gildas, who wrote On the Ruin of Britain. The Life of Gildas tells that Caw’s family came south from the North to Wales, and several of his sons are remembered in Ynys Môn, where the cantref of Twrcelyn holds the churches of Saint Eugrad at Llaneugrad, Saint Gallgo at Llanallgo and Saint Cwyllog at Llangwyllog. Welsh tradition counts the poet Aneirin among his descendants too.",
    "In Culhwch ac Olwen Caw is one of Arthur’s warriors, and the last of the giant’s tasks falls to him. When every wonder has been won, it is Caw of Prydyn who comes with the razor and shaves the giant Ysbaddaden, so that Culhwch can at last win Olwen."],
   ["Arglwydd o’r Gogledd oedd Caw o Brydyn, Caw o Brydain mewn rhai testunau, yn y genhedlaeth cyn y saint mawr. Mae’r Trioedd yn enwi ei deulu, gyda rhai Brychan Brycheiniog a Chunedda Wledig, yn un o Dair Gwelygordd Saint Ynys Prydain, ac mae achau Cymraeg y saint yn cyfrif mwy nag ugain o’i feibion a’i ferched.",
    "Yr enwocaf ohonynt oedd Gildas, a ysgrifennodd Am Ddinistr Prydain. Mae Buchedd Gildas yn adrodd i deulu Caw ddod o’r Gogledd i Gymru, a chofir nifer o’i feibion ym Môn, lle mae cantref Twrcelyn yn cynnwys eglwysi Eugrad Sant yn Llaneugrad, Gallgo Sant yn Llanallgo a Chwyllog Sant yn Llangwyllog. Mae traddodiad Cymru’n cyfrif y bardd Aneirin ymhlith ei ddisgynyddion hefyd.",
    "Yn Culhwch ac Olwen mae Caw yn un o ryfelwyr Arthur, ac i’w ran ef y syrth y dasg olaf. Pan enillwyd pob rhyfeddod, Caw o Brydyn sy’n dod â’r ellyn ac yn eillio’r cawr Ysbaddaden, fel y gall Culhwch o’r diwedd ennill Olwen."]),
 P("pabo-post-prydain", "saints", "fl. early 6th century", "Pabo Post Prydain", "Pabo Post Prydain", "King of the North, ‘Pillar of Britain’", "Brenin yn y Gogledd, ‘Post Prydain’", "Pabo_Post_Prydain", "rulers",
   "A king of the Old North called the Pillar of Britain, who ended his days in Ynys Môn. His church is Llanbabo, where his carved stone figure, crowned and holding a sceptre, still stands.",
   "Brenin yn yr Hen Ogledd a elwid yn Bost Prydain, a ddiweddodd ei ddyddiau ym Môn. Ei eglwys yw Llanbabo, lle saif ei ddelw garreg, â choron am ei ben a theyrnwialen yn ei law, o hyd.",
   ["Pabo was a king of the Britons of the North in the early sixth century, of the line of Coel Hen. His title, Post Prydain, means the Pillar of Britain, a name the Welsh gave only to the great defenders of the island. His sons Dunod Fawr and Sawyl Benuchel were kings of the North after him, and Dunod is named in the Triads as one of the Three Pillars of Battle.",
    "Welsh tradition holds that in his old age Pabo came to Ynys Môn, gave up the crown and founded a church in the north of the island. It is Llanbabo, near Llyn Alaw. In the church is a carved stone of the fourteenth century showing a king with a crown on his head and a sceptre in his hand, with words in Latin naming him as Pabo Post Prydain. It was found buried in the churchyard in the seventeenth century, and has stood in the church ever since."],
   ["Brenin Brythoniaid y Gogledd oedd Pabo yn nechrau’r chweched ganrif, o linach Coel Hen. Ystyr ei deitl, Post Prydain, yw Colofn Prydain, enw na roddai’r Cymry ond i amddiffynwyr mawr yr ynys. Bu ei feibion Dunod Fawr a Sawyl Benuchel yn frenhinoedd yn y Gogledd ar ei ôl, ac enwir Dunod yn y Trioedd yn un o Dri Phost Cad Ynys Prydain.",
    "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, daeth Pabo i Ynys Môn yn ei henaint, rhoi’r goron heibio a sefydlu eglwys yng ngogledd yr ynys. Llanbabo yw hi, ger Llyn Alaw. Yn yr eglwys mae carreg gerfiedig o’r bedwaredd ganrif ar ddeg yn dangos brenin â choron am ei ben a theyrnwialen yn ei law, a geiriau Lladin yn ei enwi’n Pabo Post Prydain. Fe’i cafwyd wedi’i chladdu yn y fynwent yn yr ail ganrif ar bymtheg, ac mae wedi sefyll yn yr eglwys byth er hynny."]),

 # ---------- Kings of the Old North ----------
 P("rhydderch-hael", "saints", "d. c. 614", "Rhydderch Hael", "Rhydderch Hael", "King of Ystrad Clud", "Brenin Ystrad Clud", "Rhydderch_Hael", "rulers",
   "King of Ystrad Clud, Strathclyde, from the Rock of Dumbarton. One of the Three Generous Men of the Island of Britain, a friend of Saint Columba and the patron of Saint Kentigern.",
   "Brenin Ystrad Clud o Graig Alclud. Un o Dri Hael Ynys Prydain, cyfaill i Golwmba Sant a noddwr Cyndeyrn Sant.",
   ["Rhydderch Hael, Rhydderch the Generous, ruled the Welsh-speaking kingdom of Ystrad Clud from Alclud, the Rock of the Clyde, now Dumbarton Rock. The Triads name him with Nudd and Mordaf as one of the Three Generous Men of the Island of Britain. He fought beside Urien of Rheged, Gwallog and Morgant against the Angles, and the History of the Britons names him among the four kings who besieged them on the island of Metcaud, Lindisfarne.",
    "He is one of the few Welsh kings of his age written of by an outside witness. Adomnán, abbot of Iona, wrote in his Life of Saint Columba, around 700, of ‘Roderc son of Tothail, who reigned on the Rock of Clyde’, a friend of the saint, who sent to ask Columba whether he would be killed by his enemies. Columba answered that he would die at home on his own pillow, and so he did.",
    "He was the patron of Saint Kentigern, who came home from Llanelwy at his invitation to be bishop of his kingdom, and the Welsh poems of Myrddin name him too. The Thirteen Treasures of the Island of Britain include Dyrnwyn, the sword of Rhydderch Hael: if a worthy man drew it, it would blaze with fire from hilt to point."],
   ["Rheolai Rhydderch Hael deyrnas Gymraeg ei hiaith Ystrad Clud o Alclud, Craig Clud, Craig Dumbarton heddiw. Mae’r Trioedd yn ei enwi gyda Nudd a Mordaf yn un o Dri Hael Ynys Prydain. Ymladdodd wrth ochr Urien Rheged, Gwallog a Morgant yn erbyn yr Eingl, ac mae Hanes y Brythoniaid yn ei enwi ymhlith y pedwar brenin a fu’n gwarchae arnynt ar ynys Metcaud.",
    "Mae’n un o’r ychydig frenhinoedd Cymreig o’i oes yr ysgrifennodd tyst o’r tu allan amdanynt. Ysgrifennodd Adomnán, abad Iona, yn ei Fuchedd Colwmba Sant tua 700, am ‘Roderc fab Tothail, a deyrnasai ar Graig Clud’, cyfaill i’r sant, a anfonodd i ofyn i Golwmba a fyddai ei elynion yn ei ladd. Atebodd Colwmba y byddai’n marw gartref ar ei obennydd ei hun, ac felly y bu.",
    "Ef oedd noddwr Cyndeyrn Sant, a ddaeth adref o Lanelwy ar ei wahoddiad i fod yn esgob ei deyrnas, ac mae cerddi Myrddin yn ei enwi hefyd. Mae Tri Thlws ar Ddeg Ynys Prydain yn cynnwys Dyrnwyn, cleddyf Rhydderch Hael: pe tynnai gŵr teilwng ef, fe ffaglai â thân o’r carn i’r blaen."]),
 P("gwallog", "saints", "fl. late 6th century", "Gwallog ap Lleënog", "Gwallog ap Lleënog", "King of Elmet", "Brenin Elfed", "Gwallog_ap_Lleënog", "rulers",
   "King of Elmet, the Welsh kingdom around Leeds, praised by Taliesin and named with Urien and Rhydderch among the kings who fought the Angles at Lindisfarne. The Triads count him one of the Three Pillars of Battle.",
   "Brenin Elfed, y deyrnas Gymreig o gwmpas Leeds, a folwyd gan Daliesin ac a enwir gydag Urien a Rhydderch ymhlith y brenhinoedd a ymladdodd yn erbyn yr Eingl yn Lindisfarne. Mae’r Trioedd yn ei gyfrif yn un o Dri Phost Cad.",
   ["Gwallog son of Lleënog was king of Elmet, Elfed in Welsh, a kingdom of the Britons in the valleys of west Yorkshire around Leeds, in the late sixth century. Elmet held out as a Welsh kingdom into the seventh century, long after the lands around it had fallen to the Angles, and its name still survives in villages such as Sherburn-in-Elmet.",
    "The History of the Britons names Gwallog with Urien, Rhydderch Hael and Morgant as the four kings who fought the Angles in the north, and who held them besieged on the island of Lindisfarne for three days and three nights. Taliesin sang his praise in the Book of Taliesin, and the Triads name him with Dunod son of Pabo and Cynfelyn Drwsgl as one of the Three Pillars of Battle of the Island of Britain."],
   ["Gwallog fab Lleënog oedd brenin Elfed, teyrnas Brythoniaid yng nghymoedd gorllewin swydd Efrog o gwmpas Leeds, ar ddiwedd y chweched ganrif. Daliodd Elfed yn deyrnas Gymreig i mewn i’r seithfed ganrif, ymhell ar ôl i’r tiroedd o’i chwmpas syrthio i’r Eingl, ac mae ei henw’n goroesi o hyd mewn pentrefi megis Sherburn-in-Elmet.",
    "Mae Hanes y Brythoniaid yn enwi Gwallog gydag Urien, Rhydderch Hael a Morgant fel y pedwar brenin a ymladdodd yn erbyn yr Eingl yn y gogledd, ac a fu’n gwarchae arnynt ar ynys Metcaud am dri diwrnod a thair noson. Canodd Taliesin ei glod yn Llyfr Taliesin, ac mae’r Trioedd yn ei enwi gyda Dunod fab Pabo a Chynfelyn Drwsgl yn un o Dri Phost Cad Ynys Prydain."]),
 P("gwenddolau", "saints", "d. 573", "Gwenddolau ap Ceidio", "Gwenddolau ap Ceidio", "King in the Old North, Myrddin’s lord", "Brenin yn yr Hen Ogledd, arglwydd Myrddin", "Gwenddoleu_ap_Ceidio", "rulers",
   "King in the Old North who fell at the battle of Arfderydd in 573. His war band fought on for six weeks after his death, and Myrddin, his bard, mourned him in the old poems.",
   "Brenin yn yr Hen Ogledd a syrthiodd ym mrwydr Arfderydd yn 573. Ymladdodd ei osgordd ymlaen am chwe wythnos wedi ei farw, a galarodd Myrddin, ei fardd, amdano yn yr hen gerddi.",
   ["Gwenddolau son of Ceidio was a king of the Britons of the North, of the line of Coel Hen, in the country around the Esk north of Carlisle, where the name Carwinley, Caer Wenddolau, still marks his fortress. The Welsh Annals record that in 573 he fell at the battle of Arfderydd, Arthuret.",
    "The Triads honour his men as one of the Three Faithful War-Bands of the Island of Britain: after their lord was slain, the war band of Gwenddolau fought on at Arfderydd for a fortnight and a month. The Thirteen Treasures of the Island of Britain include his gwyddbwyll board, of gold with pieces of silver, on which the pieces would play by themselves.",
    "Welsh tradition holds that Myrddin was Gwenddolau’s bard. In the poems of the Black Book of Carmarthen, Myrddin remembers his lord with love: he wore a gold torque in battle, and Myrddin, after Arfderydd, went out into the forest of Celyddon grieving for him."],
   ["Brenin Brythoniaid y Gogledd oedd Gwenddolau fab Ceidio, o linach Coel Hen, yn y wlad o gwmpas afon Esk i’r gogledd o Gaerliwelydd, lle mae’r enw Carwinley, Caer Wenddolau, yn dal i nodi ei gaer. Mae Blwyddnodion Cymru’n cofnodi iddo syrthio ym mrwydr Arfderydd yn 573.",
    "Mae’r Trioedd yn anrhydeddu ei wŷr fel un o Dair Gosgordd Ffyddlon Ynys Prydain: wedi lladd eu harglwydd, ymladdodd gosgordd Gwenddolau ymlaen yn Arfderydd am bythefnos a mis. Mae Tri Thlws ar Ddeg Ynys Prydain yn cynnwys ei wyddbwyll, o aur a’r gwŷr o arian, lle chwaraeai’r gwŷr ar eu pennau eu hunain.",
    "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, Myrddin oedd bardd Gwenddolau. Yng ngherddi Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin mae Myrddin yn cofio ei arglwydd â chariad: gwisgai dorch aur mewn brwydr, ac aeth Myrddin, wedi Arfderydd, allan i Goed Celyddon yn galaru amdano."]),
 P("clydno-eidyn", "saints", "fl. mid 6th century", "Clydno Eidyn", "Clydno Eidyn", "King of Eidyn in the Old North", "Brenin Eidyn yn yr Hen Ogledd", "Clydno_Eidyn", "rulers",
   "A king of the Britons of Eidyn, the country of Edinburgh, one of the Men of the North. His son Cynon rode to Catraeth, and his halter is one of the Thirteen Treasures of the Island of Britain.",
   "Brenin Brythoniaid Eidyn, gwlad Caeredin, un o Wŷr y Gogledd. Marchogodd ei fab Cynon i Gatraeth, ac mae ei gebystr yn un o Dri Thlws ar Ddeg Ynys Prydain.",
   ["Clydno Eidyn was one of the Gwŷr y Gogledd, the Men of the North, the kings of the Britons between the Pennines and the Forth, and he ruled in Eidyn, the country around Din Eidyn, Edinburgh, in the middle of the sixth century. The Welsh law books name him with Rhydderch Hael, Nudd Hael and Mordaf Hael among the kings of the North who rode south to Arfon to avenge Elidir Mwynfawr.",
    "His son Cynon was one of the heroes of Y Gododdin, the war band that rode from Din Eidyn to Catraeth, and one of the few who came home. The Thirteen Treasures of the Island of Britain include the halter of Clydno Eidyn: fixed to a staple at the foot of his bed, it would hold whatever horse he wished for in the morning."],
   ["Roedd Clydno Eidyn yn un o Wŷr y Gogledd, brenhinoedd y Brythoniaid rhwng y Pennines a Gwerydd, a rheolai yn Eidyn, y wlad o gwmpas Din Eidyn, Caeredin, yng nghanol y chweched ganrif. Mae llyfrau cyfraith Cymru’n ei enwi gyda Rhydderch Hael, Nudd Hael a Mordaf Hael ymhlith brenhinoedd y Gogledd a farchogodd i Arfon i ddial Elidir Mwynfawr.",
    "Roedd ei fab Cynon yn un o arwyr Y Gododdin, yr osgordd a farchogodd o Ddin Eidyn i Gatraeth, ac yn un o’r ychydig a ddaeth adref. Mae Tri Thlws ar Ddeg Ynys Prydain yn cynnwys cebystr Clydno Eidyn: wedi’i glymu wrth stwffwl wrth draed ei wely, daliai ba bynnag farch a fynnai yn y bore."]),
 P("mynyddog-mwynfawr", "saints", "fl. c. 600", "Mynyddog Mwynfawr", "Mynyddog Mwynfawr", "Lord of Din Eidyn, who feasted the Gododdin", "Arglwydd Din Eidyn, a wleddodd y Gododdin", "Mynyddog_Mwynfawr", "rulers",
   "The lord of Din Eidyn who gathered the war band of Y Gododdin and feasted them on mead for a year before they rode to Catraeth.",
   "Arglwydd Din Eidyn a gasglodd osgordd Y Gododdin a’u gwledda ar fedd am flwyddyn cyn iddynt farchogaeth i Gatraeth.",
   ["Mynyddog Mwynfawr, Mynyddog the Wealthy or the Generous, was the lord of Din Eidyn, the fortress of Edinburgh, in the time of Aneirin. Y Gododdin, Aneirin’s great poem and the oldest poetry of Britain in a language still spoken, tells how he gathered the finest young warriors from across the lands of the Britons, from Gwynedd to the North.",
    "For a year they feasted in his hall on mead and wine, and then, in about 600, he sent them south to Catraeth, Catterick, against the Angles of Deira and Bernicia. ‘They paid for their mead,’ the poem says again and again: they repaid their lord’s feast with their courage. Mynyddog is remembered as the generous lord whose hall was the heart of the last great stand of the Britons of the North."],
   ["Mynyddog Mwynfawr oedd arglwydd Din Eidyn, caer Caeredin, yn oes Aneirin. Mae’r Gododdin, cerdd fawr Aneirin a barddoniaeth hynaf Prydain mewn iaith a siaredir o hyd, yn adrodd sut y casglodd y rhyfelwyr ifanc gorau o diroedd y Brythoniaid, o Wynedd i’r Gogledd.",
    "Am flwyddyn buont yn gwledda yn ei neuadd ar fedd a gwin, ac yna, tua 600, anfonodd hwy tua’r de i Gatraeth yn erbyn Eingl Deifr a Bryneich. ‘Talu eu medd’ a wnaethant, medd y gerdd dro ar ôl tro: talasant am wledd eu harglwydd â’u dewrder. Fe gofir Mynyddog fel yr arglwydd hael yr oedd ei neuadd yn galon safiad mawr olaf Brythoniaid y Gogledd."]),
 P("llywarch-hen", "saints", "fl. late 6th century", "Llywarch Hen", "Llywarch Hen", "King of the North, the old man of the poems", "Brenin yn y Gogledd, hen ŵr y cerddi", "Llywarch_Hen", "rulers",
   "A king of the Old North, cousin of Urien Rheged, whose name the old englynion of Canu Llywarch Hen made immortal. Welsh tradition holds that he ended his days at Llanfor near Bala.",
   "Brenin yn yr Hen Ogledd, cefnder Urien Rheged, y gwnaeth hen englynion Canu Llywarch Hen ei enw’n anfarwol. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru diweddodd ei ddyddiau yn Llanfor ger y Bala.",
   ["Llywarch Hen, Llywarch the Old, was a king of the Britons of the North in the late sixth century, of the line of Coel Hen and a cousin of Urien Rheged. Welsh tradition holds that he had twenty-four sons, every one of whom wore a gold torque and was a leader of men.",
    "He lives in the englynion of Canu Llywarch Hen, some of the finest poetry in Welsh, written down from about the ninth century. In them Llywarch has lost his lands in the North and come south to Powys. He urges his sons to stand and fight, and one by one they fall. The poems mourn Gwên, his son, who kept watch at the ford of Morlas, and end with Cân yr Henwr, the Song of the Old Man, one of the great poems of old age in any language: ‘Before I was bent-backed, I was bold of speech.’",
    "Welsh tradition holds that he lived to a great age and died at Llanfor, near Bala, where he is said to be buried, and the list of Arthur’s twenty-four knights names him as one of the Three Counselling Knights of Arthur’s court."],
   ["Brenin Brythoniaid y Gogledd oedd Llywarch Hen ar ddiwedd y chweched ganrif, o linach Coel Hen a chefnder i Urien Rheged. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru roedd ganddo bedwar mab ar hugain, a phob un yn gwisgo torch aur ac yn arweinydd gwŷr.",
    "Mae’n fyw yn englynion Canu Llywarch Hen, peth o’r farddoniaeth orau yn y Gymraeg, a ysgrifennwyd o tua’r nawfed ganrif. Ynddynt mae Llywarch wedi colli ei diroedd yn y Gogledd ac wedi dod i’r de i Bowys. Mae’n annog ei feibion i sefyll ac ymladd, ac fesul un maent yn syrthio. Mae’r cerddi’n galaru am Wên, ei fab, a gadwodd wyliadwriaeth wrth ryd Morlas, ac yn gorffen â Chân yr Henwr, un o gerddi mawr henaint mewn unrhyw iaith: ‘Cyn bum cefngrwm, bum hy fy ngeiriau.’",
    "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru bu fyw i oedran mawr a bu farw yn Llanfor, ger y Bala, lle dywedir ei fod wedi’i gladdu, ac mae rhestr pedwar marchog ar hugain Arthur yn ei enwi’n un o Dri Chynghoriad Farchog llys Arthur."]),
 P("gwyddno-garanhir", "saints", "fl. 6th century", "Gwyddno Garanhir", "Gwyddno Garanhir", "King of Cantre’r Gwaelod", "Brenin Cantre’r Gwaelod", "Gwyddno_Garanhir", "rulers",
   "The king of Cantre’r Gwaelod, the drowned land beneath Cardigan Bay, in Welsh tradition. His hamper was one of the Thirteen Treasures, and in his weir his son Elffin found the baby Taliesin.",
   "Brenin Cantre’r Gwaelod, y tir a foddwyd dan Fae Ceredigion, yn nhraddodiad Cymru. Roedd ei fwys yn un o’r Tri Thlws ar Ddeg, ac yn ei gored y cafodd ei fab Elffin y baban Taliesin.",
   ["Gwyddno Garanhir, Gwyddno Long-Shank, is remembered in Welsh tradition as the king of Cantre’r Gwaelod, the Lowland Hundred, a rich country of sixteen towns that lay where Cardigan Bay now is, guarded from the sea by dykes and sluices. A poem in the Black Book of Carmarthen mourns the drowning of Maes Gwyddno, Gwyddno’s plain, when the sea broke in, and tradition holds that on a quiet day the bells of the lost churches can still be heard beneath the waves. At low tide the long stone ridge of Sarn Cynfelyn runs out to sea from near Borth.",
    "In the Black Book he speaks too, in a dialogue with Gwyn ap Nudd, king of Annwfn. The Thirteen Treasures of the Island of Britain include the hamper of Gwyddno Garanhir: put food for one man into it, and you would find food for a hundred.",
    "After the flood, tradition holds, he kept a great weir on the shore near the mouth of the Dyfi, Cored Wyddno, and it was there that his son Elffin, fishing on the eve of May, found the baby Taliesin."],
   ["Fe gofir Gwyddno Garanhir yn nhraddodiad Cymru fel brenin Cantre’r Gwaelod, gwlad gyfoethog o un dref ar bymtheg a orweddai lle mae Bae Ceredigion heddiw, wedi’i gwarchod rhag y môr gan forgloddiau a llifddorau. Mae cerdd yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin yn galaru am foddi Maes Gwyddno, pan dorrodd y môr i mewn, ac yn ôl traddodiad ar ddiwrnod tawel gellir clywed clychau’r eglwysi coll dan y tonnau o hyd. Ar drai mae crib hir garegog Sarn Cynfelyn yn rhedeg allan i’r môr o ymyl y Borth.",
    "Yn y Llyfr Du mae’n llefaru hefyd, mewn ymddiddan â Gwyn ap Nudd, brenin Annwfn. Mae Tri Thlws ar Ddeg Ynys Prydain yn cynnwys mwys Gwyddno Garanhir: rhowch fwyd i un gŵr ynddo, a chewch fwyd i gant.",
    "Wedi’r dilyw, yn ôl traddodiad, cadwai gored fawr ar y lan ger aber Dyfi, Cored Wyddno, ac yno y cafodd ei fab Elffin, wrth bysgota ar nos Galan Mai, y baban Taliesin."]),
 P("elffin", "saints", "fl. 6th century", "Elffin ap Gwyddno", "Elffin ap Gwyddno", "The prince who found Taliesin", "Y tywysog a gafodd Daliesin", "Elffin_ap_Gwyddno", "heroes",
   "Son of Gwyddno Garanhir, who found the baby Taliesin in his father’s weir and raised him. When Elffin was held at Maelgwn Gwynedd’s court, Taliesin set him free by the power of his poetry.",
   "Mab Gwyddno Garanhir, a gafodd y baban Taliesin yng nghored ei dad a’i fagu. Pan ddaliwyd Elffin yn llys Maelgwn Gwynedd, rhyddhaodd Taliesin ef drwy rym ei farddoniaeth.",
   ["Elffin was the son of Gwyddno Garanhir. Hanes Taliesin, the Tale of Taliesin, tells that he was thought the unluckiest young man in the land, until one eve of May his father gave him the catch of the weir. There was not a single fish in it, only a leather bag caught on a pole. Elffin opened it and saw the forehead of a baby boy. ‘Behold a radiant brow!’ he cried, tal iesin, and that became the child’s name: Taliesin.",
    "Elffin carried the baby home on his horse, and the child sang him a poem of comfort on the way, Dyhuddiant Elffin, ‘Fair Elffin, cease your weeping’. Elffin and his wife raised him as their own son, and from that day Elffin’s luck turned.",
    "Years later Elffin boasted at the court of Maelgwn Gwynedd at Deganwy that his wife was as virtuous as any queen and that his bard was wiser than all the king’s bards, and Maelgwn threw him in chains. Taliesin came to Deganwy, cast a spell on the king’s twenty-four bards so that they could only play ‘blerwm, blerwm’ on their lips, and then sang so wonderfully that Maelgwn set Elffin free."],
   ["Mab Gwyddno Garanhir oedd Elffin. Mae Hanes Taliesin yn adrodd y tybid mai ef oedd y llanc anlwcaf yn y wlad, nes i’w dad roi iddo, un nos Galan Mai, helfa’r gored. Nid oedd un pysgodyn ynddi, dim ond cwdyn lledr wedi’i ddal ar bolyn. Agorodd Elffin ef a gweld talcen bachgen bach. ‘Dyma dal iesin!’ meddai, ac felly y cafodd y plentyn ei enw: Taliesin.",
    "Cariodd Elffin y baban adref ar ei farch, a chanodd y plentyn gerdd o gysur iddo ar y ffordd, Dyhuddiant Elffin, ‘Elffin deg, taw â’th wylo’. Magodd Elffin a’i wraig ef fel eu mab eu hunain, ac o’r dydd hwnnw troes lwc Elffin.",
    "Flynyddoedd yn ddiweddarach brolio a wnaeth Elffin yn llys Maelgwn Gwynedd yn Negannwy fod ei wraig mor rinweddol ag unrhyw frenhines ac mai ei fardd oedd yn ddoethach na holl feirdd y brenin, a thaflodd Maelgwn ef i gadwynau. Daeth Taliesin i Ddegannwy, bwrw hud ar bedwar bardd ar hugain y brenin fel na allent ond chwarae ‘blerwm, blerwm’ ar eu gwefusau, ac yna canu mor rhyfeddol nes i Faelgwn ryddhau Elffin."]),

 # ---------- Saints of the sixth century ----------
 P("brynach", "saints", "fl. c. 540", "Saint Brynach", "Sant Brynach", "The saint of Nevern", "Sant Nanhyfer", "Saint_Brynach", "faith",
   "A saint from Ireland who made his home at Nevern in Pembrokeshire. His Life tells that he climbed Carn Ingli to speak with angels, and the great carved cross of Nevern stands in his churchyard.",
   "Sant o Iwerddon a wnaeth ei gartref yn Nanhyfer yn Sir Benfro. Medd ei Fuchedd iddo ddringo Carn Ingli i siarad ag angylion, a saif croes gerfiedig fawr Nanhyfer yn ei fynwent.",
   ["Brynach Wyddel, Brynach the Irishman, came to Wales after travelling to Rome and Brittany, his Life says, and settled in the valley of the Nyfer. He was a friend of Dewi Sant, and the Brecon genealogies say he married Corth, one of the daughters of Brychan Brycheiniog. His Life tells that he would climb Carn Ingli, the Mount of Angels, above Newport, to pray and to speak with the angels there.",
    "His church at Nevern, Nanhyfer, is one of the great holy places of Wales. In the churchyard stands the Nevern Cross, more than four metres tall and carved with knotwork, one of the finest stone crosses in Wales, and nearby the Vitalianus stone with its inscription in Latin and ogam. Tradition holds that the first cuckoo of spring sings from the top of the cross on Brynach’s feast day, 7 April, and the old yew by the path, the bleeding yew, drips red sap. Llanfrynach in Brycheiniog also carries his name."],
   ["Daeth Brynach Wyddel i Gymru wedi teithio i Rufain a Llydaw, medd ei Fuchedd, ac ymsefydlu yn nyffryn Nyfer. Roedd yn gyfaill i Ddewi Sant, ac mae achau Brycheiniog yn dweud iddo briodi Corth, un o ferched Brychan Brycheiniog. Mae ei Fuchedd yn adrodd y dringai Garn Ingli, mynydd yr angylion, uwchben Trefdraeth, i weddïo ac i siarad â’r angylion yno.",
    "Mae ei eglwys yn Nanhyfer yn un o fannau sanctaidd mawr Cymru. Yn y fynwent saif Croes Nanhyfer, dros bedwar metr o uchder ac wedi’i cherfio â chlymwaith, un o’r croesau carreg gorau yng Nghymru, a gerllaw maen Vitalianus â’i arysgrif mewn Lladin ac ogam. Yn ôl traddodiad mae cog gyntaf y gwanwyn yn canu o ben y groes ar ddydd gŵyl Brynach, 7 Ebrill, ac mae’r ywen hen wrth y llwybr, yr ywen sy’n gwaedu, yn diferu sudd coch. Mae Llanfrynach ym Mrycheiniog hefyd yn dwyn ei enw."]),
 P("dogfael", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Saint Dogfael", "Sant Dogfael", "The saint of Llandudoch, St Dogmaels", "Sant Llandudoch", "Saint_Dogmael", "faith",
   "A saint of the line of Cunedda who founded the church at the mouth of the Teifi. St Dogmaels Abbey later rose on the same ground, and its ogam stone helped scholars read ogam for the first time.",
   "Sant o linach Cunedda a sefydlodd yr eglwys wrth aber Teifi. Cododd Abaty Llandudoch yn ddiweddarach ar yr un tir, a helpodd ei garreg ogam ysgolheigion i ddarllen ogam am y tro cyntaf.",
   ["Dogfael was the son of Ithel son of Ceredig, of the royal line of Cunedda that gave Ceredigion its name. He founded his church on the south bank of the Teifi, near its mouth, at the place the Welsh call Llandudoch, and the English St Dogmaels. Churches in Pembrokeshire and Ynys Môn carry his name, and he is remembered in Brittany too. His feast day is 14 June.",
    "Centuries later a great Benedictine abbey was built on the site, and its ruins still stand. In the church is the Sagranus stone, carved in the sixth century with the same name in Latin letters and in ogam. Because it gives the name in both scripts, it was the key that let scholars in 1848 confirm how the ogam alphabet should be read."],
   ["Mab Ithel fab Ceredig oedd Dogfael, o linach frenhinol Cunedda a roddodd ei henw i Geredigion. Sefydlodd ei eglwys ar lan ddeheuol Teifi, ger ei haber, yn y lle a eilw’r Cymry Llandudoch, a’r Saeson St Dogmaels. Mae eglwysi yn Sir Benfro ac ym Môn yn dwyn ei enw, ac fe’i cofir yn Llydaw hefyd. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 14 Mehefin.",
    "Ganrifoedd yn ddiweddarach codwyd abaty Benedictaidd mawr ar y safle, ac mae ei adfeilion yn sefyll o hyd. Yn yr eglwys mae carreg Sagranus, a gerfiwyd yn y chweched ganrif â’r un enw mewn llythrennau Lladin ac mewn ogam. Gan ei bod yn rhoi’r enw yn y ddwy sgript, hi oedd yr allwedd a alluogodd ysgolheigion yn 1848 i gadarnhau sut y dylid darllen yr wyddor ogam."]),
 P("gofan", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Saint Gofan", "Sant Gofan", "The hermit of the cliffs", "Meudwy’r clogwyni", "St_Govan%27s_Chapel", "faith",
   "A hermit of the Pembrokeshire cliffs whose tiny stone chapel is wedged into a cleft halfway down the cliff near Bosherston. Some Welsh tradition holds that Gofan was Gwalchmai, Arthur’s nephew.",
   "Meudwy clogwyni Sir Benfro y mae ei gapel carreg bach wedi’i wasgu i hollt hanner ffordd i lawr y clogwyn ger Bosherston. Yn ôl peth traddodiad Cymreig, Gwalchmai, nai Arthur, oedd Gofan.",
   ["On the south coast of Pembrokeshire, near Bosherston, a flight of stone steps leads down the cliff to a tiny chapel built across a cleft in the rock, just above the sea. This is Capel Gofan, St Govan’s Chapel. The chapel that stands today was built in the thirteenth century on the site of a much older cell, and tradition holds that no one counts the same number of steps going down as coming up.",
    "Tradition tells that Gofan was a holy man who lived here as a hermit. When pirates came ashore, the rock opened to hide him and closed around him until they had gone, and the marks of his body can still be seen in the stone. A holy well below the chapel was visited for its healing water. Some tellers hold that Gofan was an Irish abbot; others, in Welsh tradition, that he was none other than Gwalchmai, Arthur’s nephew, who left the court to end his days in prayer by the sea."],
   ["Ar arfordir deheuol Sir Benfro, ger Bosherston, mae grisiau carreg yn arwain i lawr y clogwyn at gapel bach a godwyd ar draws hollt yn y graig, ychydig uwchben y môr. Capel Gofan yw hwn. Codwyd y capel sy’n sefyll heddiw yn y drydedd ganrif ar ddeg ar safle cell llawer hŷn, ac yn ôl traddodiad nid yw neb yn cyfrif yr un nifer o risiau wrth fynd i lawr ag wrth ddod i fyny.",
    "Medd y traddodiad mai gŵr sanctaidd oedd Gofan a fu’n byw yma’n feudwy. Pan ddaeth môr-ladron i’r lan, agorodd y graig i’w guddio a chau amdano nes iddynt fynd, ac mae ôl ei gorff i’w weld yn y garreg o hyd. Ymwelid â ffynnon sanctaidd islaw’r capel am ei dŵr iachusol. Mae rhai’n dal mai abad o Iwerddon oedd Gofan; eraill, yn nhraddodiad Cymru, mai neb llai na Gwalchmai, nai Arthur, oedd ef, a adawodd y llys i ddiweddu ei ddyddiau mewn gweddi ger y môr."]),
 P("cennydd", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Saint Cennydd", "Sant Cennydd", "The saint of Gower", "Sant Gŵyr", "Saint_Cenydd", "faith",
   "The saint of Gower. Gower tradition tells that as a baby he was carried by seagulls to a rock off the coast and cared for by heaven. He grew up to found the church at Llangennith.",
   "Sant Gŵyr. Medd traddodiad Gŵyr iddo, yn faban, gael ei gario gan wylanod i graig oddi ar yr arfordir a’i warchod gan y nef. Tyfodd i sefydlu’r eglwys yn Llangynydd.",
   ["Cennydd is the saint of Gower. Gower tradition tells that as a baby he was set adrift in a wicker cradle on the estuary of the Llwchwr, and that seagulls lifted him from the water and carried him to the rock of Burry Holms, off the north-west tip of the peninsula. There, it is told, he was fed by a little bell sent from heaven, and cared for by the birds and by an angel until he grew to be a man.",
    "He founded his church at Llangennith, Llangynydd, below Rhossili Down, and it is the largest church in Gower. Burry Holms, where he is said to have lived as a hermit, still has the remains of an early chapel. Senghennydd in the valleys, where Cennydd is also remembered, keeps a version of his name."],
   ["Cennydd yw sant Gŵyr. Medd traddodiad Gŵyr iddo, yn faban, gael ei roi ar y dŵr mewn crud gwiail ar aber Llwchwr, ac i wylanod ei godi o’r dŵr a’i gario i graig Burry Holms, oddi ar benrhyn gogledd-orllewinol Gŵyr. Yno, medd y stori, fe’i bwydwyd gan gloch fach a anfonwyd o’r nef, a’i warchod gan yr adar a chan angel nes iddo dyfu’n ŵr.",
    "Sefydlodd ei eglwys yn Llangynydd, islaw Rhosili, a hi yw’r eglwys fwyaf ar Benrhyn Gŵyr. Mae olion capel cynnar o hyd ar Burry Holms, lle dywedir iddo fyw’n feudwy. Mae Senghennydd yn y cymoedd, lle cofir Cennydd hefyd, yn cadw ffurf ar ei enw."]),
 P("elli", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Saint Elli", "Sant Elli", "Cadog’s pupil, the saint of Llanelli", "Disgybl Cadog, sant Llanelli", "Elli_(saint)", "faith",
   "A pupil of Saint Cadog at Llancarfan, who followed him as abbot. The town of Llanelli is named after him.",
   "Disgybl i Gadog Sant yn Llancarfan, a’i dilynodd yn abad. Enwyd tref Llanelli ar ei ôl.",
   ["Elli was the favourite pupil of Saint Cadog at Llancarfan, the great monastery of the Vale of Glamorgan. The Life of Cadog tells how the master loved him, and when Cadog left Wales at the end of his life, it was Elli who followed him as abbot of Llancarfan.",
    "His church on the Llwchwr estuary gave its name to the town of Llanelli, and Llanelli in Gwent, near Crickhowell, carries it too. From his church grew one of the great towns of industrial Wales, the home of tinplate and of the Scarlets, and every time its name is spoken, the saint of Llancarfan is remembered."],
   ["Elli oedd hoff ddisgybl Cadog Sant yn Llancarfan, mynachlog fawr Bro Morgannwg. Mae Buchedd Cadog yn adrodd fel y carai’r athro ef, a phan adawodd Cadog Gymru ar ddiwedd ei oes, Elli a’i dilynodd yn abad Llancarfan.",
    "Rhoddodd ei eglwys ar aber Llwchwr ei henw i dref Llanelli, ac mae Llanelli yng Ngwent, ger Crucywel, yn ei ddwyn hefyd. O’i eglwys tyfodd un o drefi mawr y Gymru ddiwydiannol, cartref y tunplat a’r Scarlets, a phob tro y dywedir ei henw, cofir sant Llancarfan."]),
 P("tysul", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Saint Tysul", "Sant Tysul", "The saint of Llandysul", "Sant Llandysul", "Saint_Tysul", "faith",
   "A saint of the royal line of Cunedda and a cousin of Dewi Sant. The market town of Llandysul on the Teifi carries his name.",
   "Sant o linach frenhinol Cunedda a chefnder i Ddewi Sant. Mae tref farchnad Llandysul ar Deifi yn dwyn ei enw.",
   ["Tysul was the son of Corun son of Ceredig, of the line of Cunedda, and so a cousin of Dewi Sant. He founded his church in the valley of the Teifi, where the market town of Llandysul grew up around it, and another at Llandyssil in Montgomeryshire, which carries his name too.",
    "Llandysul became one of the great centres of Welsh life in the Teifi valley: a town of woollen mills, of chapels and of Welsh printing, where Gwasg Gomer was founded in 1892. The saint of the line of Cunedda gave his name to a town that has served the Welsh language for generations."],
   ["Mab Corun fab Ceredig oedd Tysul, o linach Cunedda, ac felly’n gefnder i Ddewi Sant. Sefydlodd ei eglwys yn nyffryn Teifi, lle tyfodd tref farchnad Llandysul o’i chwmpas, ac un arall yn Llandysul ym Maldwyn, sy’n dwyn ei enw hefyd.",
    "Daeth Llandysul yn un o ganolfannau mawr bywyd Cymreig yn nyffryn Teifi: tref y melinau gwlân, y capeli a’r argraffu Cymraeg, lle sefydlwyd Gwasg Gomer yn 1892. Rhoddodd sant llinach Cunedda ei enw i dref sydd wedi gwasanaethu’r Gymraeg ers cenedlaethau."]),
 P("seiriol", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Saint Seiriol", "Sant Seiriol", "The saint of Penmon, Seiriol the Fair", "Sant Penmon, Seiriol Wyn", "Saint_Seiriol", "faith",
   "Founder of the monastery at Penmon, at the eastern tip of Ynys Môn, and a cell on Ynys Seiriol. He and his friend Cybi met each week in the middle of Môn, and so became Seiriol Wyn and Cybi Felyn.",
   "Sylfaenydd y fynachlog ym Mhenmon, ym mhen dwyreiniol Ynys Môn, a chell ar Ynys Seiriol. Cyfarfyddai ef a’i gyfaill Cybi bob wythnos yng nghanol Môn, ac felly y daethant yn Seiriol Wyn a Chybi Felyn.",
   ["Seiriol was a prince of the house of Cunedda, brother of Einion Frenin, king of Llŷn, and a cousin of Maelgwn Gwynedd. He founded his monastery at Penmon, at the eastern tip of Ynys Môn, looking across the Menai to Eryri. His holy well still flows beside the ruins of the later priory, and he kept a cell for prayer on Ynys Seiriol, Puffin Island, across the sound. His feast day is 1 February.",
    "His great friend was Saint Cybi of Caergybi, Holyhead, at the other end of Môn. Tradition tells that the two saints would meet each week at the wells of Clorach, near Llannerch-y-medd, halfway between their churches. Cybi walked east in the morning and west in the evening, with the sun in his face both ways, and grew brown; Seiriol walked with the sun at his back, and stayed fair. So Môn has called them ever since Cybi Felyn and Seiriol Wyn, Cybi the Tawny and Seiriol the Fair."],
   ["Tywysog o dŷ Cunedda oedd Seiriol, brawd Einion Frenin, brenin Llŷn, a chefnder i Faelgwn Gwynedd. Sefydlodd ei fynachlog ym Mhenmon, ym mhen dwyreiniol Ynys Môn, yn edrych dros y Fenai at Eryri. Mae ei ffynnon sanctaidd yn llifo o hyd wrth adfeilion y priordy diweddarach, a chadwai gell i weddïo ar Ynys Seiriol, dros y swnt. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 1 Chwefror.",
    "Ei gyfaill mawr oedd Cybi Sant o Gaergybi, ym mhen arall Môn. Medd y traddodiad y cyfarfyddai’r ddau sant bob wythnos wrth ffynhonnau Clorach, ger Llannerch-y-medd, hanner ffordd rhwng eu heglwysi. Cerddai Cybi tua’r dwyrain yn y bore a thua’r gorllewin gyda’r hwyr, a’r haul yn ei wyneb y ddwy ffordd, a melynodd; cerddai Seiriol a’r haul ar ei gefn, ac arhosodd yn wyn. Felly y galwodd Môn hwy byth er hynny yn Gybi Felyn a Seiriol Wyn."]),
 P("cadfan", "saints", "fl. c. 516", "Saint Cadfan", "Sant Cadfan", "Founder of Tywyn and first abbot of Enlli", "Sylfaenydd Tywyn ac abad cyntaf Enlli", "Saint_Cadfan", "faith",
   "He came from Brittany with a band of companions, founded the church at Tywyn and became the first abbot of Ynys Enlli. The Cadfan Stone in his church carries the oldest known writing in Welsh.",
   "Daeth o Lydaw gyda mintai o gymdeithion, sefydlodd yr eglwys yn Nhywyn a daeth yn abad cyntaf Ynys Enlli. Mae Carreg Cadfan yn ei eglwys yn dwyn yr ysgrifen hynaf y gwyddys amdani yn y Gymraeg.",
   ["Cadfan was a Breton prince, son of Eneas Ledewig and Gwen Teirbron, and Welsh tradition holds that in the early sixth century he crossed the sea to Wales with a band of saints, his kinsmen and companions, among them Padarn, Tydecho, Hywyn, Cynon and Mael. He founded the church of Tywyn in Meirionnydd, by the mouth of the Dysynni, and then crossed to Ynys Enlli, Bardsey, where he founded the monastery and became its first abbot. Enlli became the island of twenty thousand saints. His feast day is 1 November.",
    "In his church at Tywyn stands the Cadfan Stone, a tall pillar inscribed on all four sides. Its words are in Welsh, and they are the oldest known writing in the Welsh language: proof that Welsh was being carved in stone more than twelve hundred years ago. Llangadfan in Powys also carries his name."],
   ["Tywysog o Lydaw oedd Cadfan, mab Eneas Ledewig a Gwen Teirbron, ac yn ôl traddodiad Cymru croesodd y môr i Gymru yn nechrau’r chweched ganrif gyda mintai o saint, ei geraint a’i gymdeithion, yn eu plith Padarn, Tydecho, Hywyn, Cynon a Mael. Sefydlodd eglwys Tywyn ym Meirionnydd, wrth aber Dysynni, ac yna croesi i Ynys Enlli, lle sefydlodd y fynachlog a dod yn abad cyntaf iddi. Daeth Enlli yn ynys yr ugain mil o saint. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 1 Tachwedd.",
    "Yn ei eglwys yn Nhywyn saif Carreg Cadfan, colofn dal ag arysgrif ar bob un o’i phedair ochr. Cymraeg yw ei geiriau, a dyma’r ysgrifen hynaf y gwyddys amdani yn yr iaith Gymraeg: prawf fod Cymraeg yn cael ei naddu mewn carreg fwy na deuddeg can mlynedd yn ôl. Mae Llangadfan ym Mhowys hefyd yn dwyn ei enw."]),
 P("eilian", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Saint Eilian", "Sant Eilian", "The saint of Llaneilian", "Sant Llaneilian", "Saint_Eilian", "faith",
   "The saint of Llaneilian on the north coast of Ynys Môn. Tradition tells that King Cadwallon Lawhir gave him as much land as his tame deer could run around in a day.",
   "Sant Llaneilian ar arfordir gogleddol Ynys Môn. Medd y traddodiad i’r Brenin Cadwallon Lawhir roi iddo gymaint o dir ag y gallai ei garw dof redeg o’i gwmpas mewn diwrnod.",
   ["Eilian came to Ynys Môn by sea, tradition says, with his family and his cattle, and landed at Porth yr Ychen, the harbour of the oxen, on the north coast. King Cadwallon Lawhir of Gwynedd welcomed him, and gave him as much land as his tame deer could run around in a single day. The deer ran far, and the lands of Llaneilian were wide. His feast day is 13 January.",
    "His church at Llaneilian, near Amlwch, is one of the treasures of Môn, with a twelfth-century tower and a rare painted rood loft and screen of the fifteenth century. Llanelian-yn-Rhos near Colwyn Bay carries his name too."],
   ["Daeth Eilian i Ynys Môn dros y môr, medd y traddodiad, gyda’i deulu a’i wartheg, a glanio ym Mhorth yr Ychen ar yr arfordir gogleddol. Croesawodd y Brenin Cadwallon Lawhir o Wynedd ef, a rhoi iddo gymaint o dir ag y gallai ei garw dof redeg o’i gwmpas mewn un diwrnod. Rhedodd y carw ymhell, ac roedd tiroedd Llaneilian yn eang. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 13 Ionawr.",
    "Mae ei eglwys yn Llaneilian, ger Amlwch, yn un o drysorau Môn, â thŵr o’r ddeuddegfed ganrif a llofft y grog a sgrin wedi’u peintio o’r bymthegfed ganrif, sy’n brin iawn. Mae Llanelian-yn-Rhos ger Bae Colwyn yn dwyn ei enw hefyd."]),
 P("tudno", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Saint Tudno", "Sant Tudno", "The saint of the Great Orme", "Sant y Gogarth", "Saint_Tudno", "faith",
   "A hermit who lived in a cave on the Great Orme, Y Gogarth. His little church still stands high on the headland, and the town of Llandudno takes its name from him.",
   "Meudwy a fu’n byw mewn ogof ar y Gogarth. Mae ei eglwys fach yn sefyll o hyd yn uchel ar y pentir, ac mae tref Llandudno yn cymryd ei henw oddi wrtho.",
   ["Tudno came to the Gogarth, the Great Orme, in the sixth century and lived as a hermit in a cave on the headland, Ogof Llech, open to the sea. Tradition holds that he was one of the many saints of the Welsh coast whose families had come from the drowned lands of Cardigan Bay. His feast day is 5 June.",
    "On the high ground of the Orme he built a little church, and St Tudno’s Church still stands there, rebuilt in the twelfth century, with its graveyard looking out over the sea. In summer, services are held in the open air beside it. When the Victorians built the great resort on the bay below, they kept the saint’s name: Llandudno, the church of Tudno."],
   ["Daeth Tudno i’r Gogarth yn y chweched ganrif a byw’n feudwy mewn ogof ar y pentir, Ogof Llech, yn agored i’r môr. Yn ôl traddodiad roedd yn un o’r llu saint ar arfordir Cymru y daethai eu teuluoedd o diroedd boddedig Bae Ceredigion. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 5 Mehefin.",
    "Ar dir uchel y Gogarth cododd eglwys fach, ac mae Eglwys Sant Tudno yn sefyll yno o hyd, wedi’i hailgodi yn y ddeuddegfed ganrif, â’i mynwent yn edrych dros y môr. Yn yr haf cynhelir gwasanaethau yn yr awyr agored wrth ei hymyl. Pan gododd y Fictoriaid y dref glan môr fawr ar y bae islaw, cadwasant enw’r sant: Llandudno, llan Tudno."]),
 P("cyndeyrn", "saints", "d. c. 612", "Saint Kentigern", "Cyndeyrn Sant", "Founder of Llanelwy and Glasgow", "Sylfaenydd Llanelwy a Glasgow", "Saint_Mungo", "faith",
   "A Briton of the Old North, grandson of a king. Welsh tradition holds that he founded the monastery of Llanelwy with nearly a thousand monks before going home to found the church of Glasgow, where he is called Mungo.",
   "Brython o’r Hen Ogledd, ŵyr brenin. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru sefydlodd fynachlog Llanelwy gyda bron i fil o fynachod cyn mynd adref i sefydlu eglwys Glasgow, lle’i gelwir yn Mungo.",
   ["Cyndeyrn, Kentigern, was a Briton of the Old North, born in Lothian. His mother was Teneu, daughter of the king of Lothian, and his Life names his father as Ewen son of Ulien, whom Welsh tradition takes to be Owain son of Urien Rheged. He was raised by Saint Serf at Culross, who called him Mungo, ‘dear one’, and he became bishop of the Britons of the Clyde.",
    "When he was driven from his country, he came to Wales. Welsh tradition holds that he visited Dewi at Mynyw, and then founded a great monastery on the river Elwy in north-east Wales, with nearly a thousand monks, who prayed in turns so that the praise of God never stopped by day or night. When King Rhydderch Hael called him home, he left the monastery to his best pupil, Asa, and Llanelwy has been St Asaph ever since.",
    "He built his church by the Molendinar burn, and the city of Glasgow grew around it. Glasgow’s coat of arms still carries his miracles: the bird, the tree, the bell and the fish with a ring. The Welsh Annals record his death in 612. His feast day is 13 January."],
   ["Brython o’r Hen Ogledd oedd Cyndeyrn, a aned yn Lleuddiniawn. Ei fam oedd Teneu, merch brenin Lleuddiniawn, ac mae ei Fuchedd yn enwi ei dad yn Ewen fab Ulien, ac mae traddodiad Cymru’n deall mai Owain ab Urien Rheged oedd ef. Fe’i magwyd gan Serf Sant yn Culross, a’i galwodd yn Mungo, ‘anwylyd’, a daeth yn esgob Brythoniaid Clud.",
    "Pan yrrwyd ef o’i wlad, daeth i Gymru. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru ymwelodd â Dewi ym Mynyw, ac yna sefydlu mynachlog fawr ar afon Elwy yng ngogledd-ddwyrain Cymru, â bron i fil o fynachod, a weddïai yn eu tro fel na pheidiai moliant Duw ddydd na nos. Pan alwodd y Brenin Rhydderch Hael ef adref, gadawodd y fynachlog i’w ddisgybl gorau, Asa, a St Asaph fu Llanelwy yn Saesneg byth er hynny.",
    "Cododd ei eglwys wrth nant Molendinar, a thyfodd dinas Glasgow o’i chwmpas. Mae arfbais Glasgow yn dwyn ei wyrthiau o hyd: yr aderyn, y goeden, y gloch a’r pysgodyn â modrwy. Mae Blwyddnodion Cymru’n cofnodi ei farw yn 612. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 13 Ionawr."]),
 P("asa", "saints", "fl. c. 600", "Saint Asaph", "Sant Asa", "Abbot and bishop of Llanelwy", "Abad ac esgob Llanelwy", "Saint_Asaph_(bishop)", "faith",
   "Kentigern’s best pupil, who followed him as abbot and bishop of Llanelwy. The cathedral city is St Asaph in English, after him.",
   "Disgybl gorau Cyndeyrn, a’i dilynodd yn abad ac esgob Llanelwy. Mae’r ddinas gadeiriol yn St Asaph yn Saesneg, ar ei ôl ef.",
   ["Asa was a boy of noble family in north-east Wales who came to Kentigern’s monastery on the Elwy. The Life of Kentigern tells that one cold morning the master needed fire, and young Asa carried burning coals to him in the fold of his cloak, and the cloth was not burnt. Kentigern saw in this a sign of God’s favour.",
    "When Kentigern went home to the North, he chose Asa to follow him as abbot and bishop, and the church of Llanelwy has carried his name in English ever since: St Asaph, now a cathedral city, with the smallest ancient cathedral in Britain. It was there that William Morgan, bishop of St Asaph, made his Welsh Bible of 1588. Llanasa in Flintshire also carries Asa’s name. His feast day is 1 May."],
   ["Bachgen o deulu bonheddig yng ngogledd-ddwyrain Cymru oedd Asa a ddaeth i fynachlog Cyndeyrn ar afon Elwy. Mae Buchedd Cyndeyrn yn adrodd fod angen tân ar yr athro un bore oer, a chariodd yr Asa ifanc farwor llosg ato ym mhlyg ei fantell, ac ni losgwyd y brethyn. Gwelodd Cyndeyrn yn hyn arwydd o ffafr Duw.",
    "Pan aeth Cyndeyrn adref i’r Gogledd, dewisodd Asa i’w ddilyn yn abad ac esgob, ac mae eglwys Llanelwy wedi dwyn ei enw yn Saesneg byth er hynny: St Asaph, dinas gadeiriol heddiw, â’r eglwys gadeiriol hynafol leiaf ym Mhrydain. Yno y lluniodd William Morgan, esgob Llanelwy, ei Feibl Cymraeg yn 1588. Mae Llanasa yn Sir y Fflint hefyd yn dwyn enw Asa. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 1 Mai."]),
 P("euddogwy", "saints", "fl. c. 600", "Saint Euddogwy", "Sant Euddogwy", "Third founder of Llandaf", "Trydydd sylfaenydd Llandaf", "Oudoceus", "faith",
   "Nephew of Teilo and bishop of Llandaf after him. The Book of Llandaff counts Dyfrig, Teilo and Euddogwy as the three founders of the see, and many of its charters from the reign of King Meurig ap Tewdrig are made to him.",
   "Nai Teilo ac esgob Llandaf ar ei ôl. Mae Llyfr Llandaf yn cyfrif Dyfrig, Teilo ac Euddogwy yn dri sylfaenydd yr esgobaeth, ac mae llawer o’i siarteri o deyrnasiad y Brenin Meurig ap Tewdrig wedi’u gwneud iddo ef.",
   ["Euddogwy, Oudoceus in Latin, was the son of Budig, a prince of Brittany, and Anawfedd, sister of Saint Teilo. He grew up under his uncle’s care and followed Teilo as bishop. The Book of Llandaff counts three founders of the see of Llandaf: Dyfrig, Teilo and Euddogwy, and the cathedral is dedicated to all three, with Saint Peter.",
    "Many of the charters of the Book of Llandaff record gifts of land made to Bishop Euddogwy by the kings of the south-east, and above all by King Meurig ap Tewdrig, whose son Athrwys Welsh tradition holds to be Arthur. It was to Euddogwy that Meurig gave the land around Mathern, where his father Tewdrig died. In his old age Euddogwy left his see to live in prayer in the Wye valley, at the place that still carries his name, Llandogo. His feast day is 2 July."],
   ["Mab Budig, tywysog o Lydaw, ac Anawfedd, chwaer Teilo Sant, oedd Euddogwy. Tyfodd dan ofal ei ewythr a dilyn Teilo yn esgob. Mae Llyfr Llandaf yn cyfrif tri sylfaenydd esgobaeth Llandaf: Dyfrig, Teilo ac Euddogwy, ac mae’r eglwys gadeiriol wedi’i chysegru i’r tri, gyda Sant Pedr.",
    "Mae llawer o siarteri Llyfr Llandaf yn cofnodi rhoddion tir a wnaed i’r Esgob Euddogwy gan frenhinoedd y de-ddwyrain, ac yn bennaf gan y Brenin Meurig ap Tewdrig, y mae traddodiad Cymru’n dal mai ei fab Athrwys oedd Arthur. I Euddogwy y rhoddodd Meurig y tir o gwmpas Merthyr Tewdrig, lle bu farw ei dad Tewdrig. Yn ei henaint gadawodd Euddogwy ei esgobaeth i fyw mewn gweddi yn nyffryn Gwy, yn y lle sy’n dwyn ei enw o hyd, Llandogo. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 2 Gorffennaf."]),
 P("tathan", "saints", "fl. c. 500", "Saint Tathan", "Sant Tathan", "Teacher of Caerwent", "Athro Caer-went", "Tathan", "faith",
   "A saint from Ireland who founded a school inside the Roman walls of Caerwent, where Cadog was among his pupils. St Athan in the Vale of Glamorgan carries his name.",
   "Sant o Iwerddon a sefydlodd ysgol o fewn muriau Rhufeinig Caer-went, lle’r oedd Cadog ymhlith ei ddisgyblion. Mae Sain Tathan ym Mro Morgannwg yn dwyn ei enw.",
   ["Tathan was the son of an Irish king, his Life tells, who was called by an angel to cross the sea to Britain. He landed at the mouth of the Severn, and the king of Gwent, hearing of his holiness, gave him land within the old Roman walls of Caerwent, Venta Silurum, the ancient capital of the Silures.",
    "There Tathan founded a school, and among its pupils was the young Cadog, son of King Gwynllyw, who would found Llancarfan. Tathan’s school carried learning on inside the walls the Romans had built, in the heart of the kingdom of Gwent. St Athan in the Vale of Glamorgan, Sain Tathan, carries his name. His feast day is 26 December."],
   ["Mab brenin o Iwerddon oedd Tathan, medd ei Fuchedd, a alwyd gan angel i groesi’r môr i Brydain. Glaniodd wrth aber Hafren, a rhoddodd brenin Gwent, wedi clywed am ei sancteiddrwydd, dir iddo o fewn hen furiau Rhufeinig Caer-went, Venta Silurum, hen brifddinas y Silwriaid.",
    "Yno sefydlodd Tathan ysgol, ac ymhlith ei disgyblion roedd Cadog ifanc, mab y Brenin Gwynllyw, a sefydlai Lancarfan. Cariodd ysgol Tathan ddysg ymlaen o fewn y muriau a gododd y Rhufeiniaid, yng nghanol teyrnas Gwent. Mae Sain Tathan ym Mro Morgannwg yn dwyn ei enw. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 26 Rhagfyr."]),
 P("pol", "saints", "fl. c. 530", "Saint Paul Aurelian", "Sant Pol", "From Llanilltud to Saint-Pol-de-Léon", "O Lanilltud i Kastell-Paol", "Paul_Aurelian", "faith",
   "Born in Penychen in Glamorgan, his Life tells, and a pupil of Illtud with Dewi and Samson. He sailed to Brittany and founded Saint-Pol-de-Léon, which still carries his name.",
   "Fe’i ganwyd ym Mhenychen ym Morgannwg, medd ei Fuchedd, ac roedd yn ddisgybl i Illtud gyda Dewi a Samson. Hwyliodd i Lydaw a sefydlu Kastell-Paol, sy’n dwyn ei enw o hyd.",
   ["Paul Aurelian, Pol in Welsh and Breton, was the son of a lord of Penychen in Glamorgan, his Life says. The Life, written at Landévennec in Brittany in 884, is one of the oldest Lives of a Welsh saint. As a boy he was sent to Illtud at Llanilltud Fawr, and studied there with Dewi, Samson and Gildas.",
    "He crossed to Brittany with twelve companions, his Life tells, and on the Île de Batz drove out a dragon that troubled the people, leading it away with his stole. He founded the monastery that grew into the town of Saint-Pol-de-Léon, Kastell-Paol, and he is one of the Seven Founder Saints of Brittany, honoured on the great pilgrimage of the Tro Breizh. His feast day is 12 March."],
   ["Mab arglwydd ym Mhenychen ym Morgannwg oedd Pawl Aurelian, Pol yn Gymraeg a Llydaweg, medd ei Fuchedd. Ysgrifennwyd y Fuchedd yn Landévennec yn Llydaw yn 884, ac mae’n un o’r Bucheddau hynaf i sant o Gymru. Yn fachgen anfonwyd ef at Illtud yn Llanilltud Fawr, a bu’n astudio yno gyda Dewi, Samson a Gildas.",
    "Croesodd i Lydaw gyda deuddeg cydymaith, medd ei Fuchedd, ac ar Enez Vaz gyrrodd allan ddraig a flinai’r bobl, gan ei harwain ymaith â’i ystola. Sefydlodd y fynachlog a dyfodd yn dref Kastell-Paol, ac mae’n un o Saith Sant Sylfaenol Llydaw, a anrhydeddir ar bererindod fawr y Tro Breizh. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 12 Mawrth."]),
 P("tudwal", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Saint Tudwal", "Sant Tudwal", "From the islands of Llŷn to Tréguier", "O ynysoedd Llŷn i Landreger", "Tudwal", "faith",
   "A saint who lived on the islands off Abersoch that still carry his name, Ynysoedd Tudwal, before crossing to Brittany to found Tréguier. He is one of the Seven Founder Saints of Brittany.",
   "Sant a fu’n byw ar yr ynysoedd ger Abersoch sy’n dal i ddwyn ei enw, Ynysoedd Tudwal, cyn croesi i Lydaw i sefydlu Landreger. Mae’n un o Saith Sant Sylfaenol Llydaw.",
   ["Tudwal lived as a hermit on the islands off Abersoch in Llŷn, and they have carried his name ever since: Ynys Tudwal Fawr and Ynys Tudwal Fach, St Tudwal’s Islands. On the larger island stand the remains of a medieval chapel on the site of his cell.",
    "His Breton Lives tell that he crossed to Brittany with his mother Pompaea and a band of followers, and founded a monastery at Tréguier, Landreger, on the north coast, which grew into a cathedral city. He is one of the Seven Founder Saints of Brittany, with Samson, Pol, Malo, Brioc, Patern and Corentin, and so Wales gave Brittany four of its seven founders."],
   ["Bu Tudwal yn byw’n feudwy ar yr ynysoedd ger Abersoch yn Llŷn, ac maent wedi dwyn ei enw byth er hynny: Ynys Tudwal Fawr ac Ynys Tudwal Fach. Ar yr ynys fwyaf saif olion capel canoloesol ar safle ei gell.",
    "Mae ei Fucheddau Llydewig yn adrodd iddo groesi i Lydaw gyda’i fam Pompaea a mintai o ddilynwyr, a sefydlu mynachlog yn Landreger ar yr arfordir gogleddol, a dyfodd yn ddinas gadeiriol. Mae’n un o Saith Sant Sylfaenol Llydaw, gyda Samson, Pol, Malo, Brioc, Padern a Chorentin, ac felly rhoddodd Cymru i Lydaw bedwar o’i saith sylfaenydd."]),
 P("briog", "saints", "fl. c. 500", "Saint Briog", "Sant Briog", "From Ceredigion to Saint-Brieuc", "O Geredigion i Sant-Brieg", "Brioc", "faith",
   "His Breton Life tells that he was born in Ceredigion. He founded the monastery that became the city of Saint-Brieuc, and in Wales he is remembered at Llandyfriog by the Teifi.",
   "Medd ei Fuchedd Lydewig iddo gael ei eni yng Ngheredigion. Sefydlodd y fynachlog a ddaeth yn ddinas Sant-Brieg, ac yng Nghymru fe’i cofir yn Llandyfriog ger Teifi.",
   ["Briog was born in Ceredigion, his Breton Life tells, to noble parents who had heard of his coming in a dream. He was sent to study under Germanus, and became a priest, and then crossed the sea to Brittany with his followers.",
    "On the north coast of Brittany he founded a monastery, and the city that grew around it carries his name: Saint-Brieuc, Sant-Brieg in Breton. He is one of the Seven Founder Saints of Brittany, honoured on the Tro Breizh. In Wales he is remembered at Llandyfriog, by the Teifi near Newcastle Emlyn. His feast day is 1 May."],
   ["Ganed Briog yng Ngheredigion, medd ei Fuchedd Lydewig, i rieni bonheddig a glywsai am ei ddyfodiad mewn breuddwyd. Anfonwyd ef i astudio dan Garmon, a daeth yn offeiriad, ac yna croesodd y môr i Lydaw gyda’i ddilynwyr.",
    "Ar arfordir gogleddol Llydaw sefydlodd fynachlog, ac mae’r ddinas a dyfodd o’i chwmpas yn dwyn ei enw: Saint-Brieuc, Sant-Brieg yn Llydaweg. Mae’n un o Saith Sant Sylfaenol Llydaw, a anrhydeddir ar y Tro Breizh. Yng Nghymru fe’i cofir yn Llandyfriog, ar lan Teifi ger Castellnewydd Emlyn. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 1 Mai."]),
 P("malo", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Saint Malo", "Sant Malo", "From Gwent to Saint-Malo", "O Went i Sant-Maloù", "Saint_Malo_(saint)", "faith",
   "Breton tradition holds that he was born in Gwent and taught at Llancarfan. He crossed to Brittany and gave his name to the walled port of Saint-Malo.",
   "Yn ôl traddodiad Llydaw fe’i ganed yng Ngwent a’i ddysgu yn Llancarfan. Croesodd i Lydaw a rhoi ei enw i borthladd caerog Sant-Maloù.",
   ["Malo, Machutus in Latin, was born in Gwent, his Breton Lives say, and was taught at Llancarfan, the monastery of Saint Cadog. The Lives tell that he sailed the ocean with Saint Brendan in search of the Land of Promise, and that on one voyage the monks said Mass on the back of a great whale, thinking it an island.",
    "He crossed to Brittany and settled at Aleth, at the mouth of the Rance, where he became bishop. The walled port that grew on the island nearby took his name: Saint-Malo, Sant-Maloù, whose sailors later crossed every ocean of the world. He is one of the Seven Founder Saints of Brittany. His feast day is 15 November."],
   ["Ganed Malo, Machutus mewn Lladin, yng Ngwent, medd ei Fucheddau Llydewig, a’i ddysgu yn Llancarfan, mynachlog Cadog Sant. Mae’r Bucheddau’n adrodd iddo hwylio’r cefnfor gyda Brendan Sant i chwilio am Wlad yr Addewid, ac i’r mynachod, ar un fordaith, ddweud yr offeren ar gefn morfil mawr, gan dybio mai ynys ydoedd.",
    "Croesodd i Lydaw ac ymsefydlu yn Aleth, wrth aber Rance, lle daeth yn esgob. Cymerodd y porthladd caerog a dyfodd ar yr ynys gerllaw ei enw: Saint-Malo, Sant-Maloù, y croesodd ei morwyr bob cefnfor yn y byd yn ddiweddarach. Mae’n un o Saith Sant Sylfaenol Llydaw. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 15 Tachwedd."]),
 P("pedrog", "saints", "fl. c. 550", "Saint Pedrog", "Sant Pedrog", "Welsh prince and saint of Cornwall", "Tywysog o Gymro a sant Cernyw", "Petroc", "faith",
   "A prince of a Welsh royal house, his Life tells, who studied in Ireland and made Cornwall his home. Padstow and Bodmin were his, and he is one of the patron saints of Cornwall. In Wales he is remembered at Llanbedrog.",
   "Tywysog o dŷ brenhinol Cymreig, medd ei Fuchedd, a astudiodd yn Iwerddon a gwneud Cernyw’n gartref iddo. Ef biau Padstow a Bodmin, ac mae’n un o nawddsaint Cernyw. Yng Nghymru fe’i cofir yn Llanbedrog.",
   ["Pedrog, Petroc in English, was the son of a Welsh king, his Life tells, and turned from the crown to study for twenty years in Ireland. Then he crossed to Cornwall and landed at the mouth of the Camel. The place where he settled became Lannwedhenek, and later Padstow, Petroc’s Stow, Petroc’s place.",
    "He founded monasteries at Padstow and at Bodmin, where his relics were later kept, and is one of the patron saints of Cornwall and of Devon. His Life tells that a hunted stag took refuge at his feet, and he protected it. In Wales he is remembered at Llanbedrog on Llŷn, with its sandy bay, and at St Petrox in Pembrokeshire. His feast day is 4 June."],
   ["Mab brenin o Gymro oedd Pedrog, Petroc yn Saesneg, medd ei Fuchedd, a throes oddi wrth y goron i astudio am ugain mlynedd yn Iwerddon. Yna croesodd i Gernyw a glanio wrth aber afon Camel. Daeth y lle yr ymsefydlodd ynddo yn Lannwedhenek, ac yn ddiweddarach yn Padstow, lle Pedrog.",
    "Sefydlodd fynachlogydd yn Padstow ac yn Bodmin, lle cadwyd ei greiriau’n ddiweddarach, ac mae’n un o nawddsaint Cernyw a Dyfnaint. Mae ei Fuchedd yn adrodd i garw a helid lochesu wrth ei draed, ac iddo ei warchod. Yng Nghymru fe’i cofir yn Llanbedrog yn Llŷn, â’i bae tywodlyd, ac yn Sain Pedrog yn Sir Benfro. Ei ddydd gŵyl yw 4 Mehefin."]),
]

